    "lawCode": "Amd §§3242 & 4329, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4856\n\nSPONSOR: REICHLIN-MELNICK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to requiring policies and\ncontracts that provide coverage for prescription drugs to include cover-\nage of an immediate additional thirty-day supply of a prescription drug\nduring a state disaster emergency\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require policies and contracts that provide coverage for prescription\ndrugs to include coverage of an immediate additional thirty-day supply\nof a prescription drug during a state disaster emergency.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 3242 of the insurance law by adding\nsection (c).\n\nSection (c).1 amending section 3242 mandates that upon the declaration\nof a state disaster emergency policies' providing coverage for\nprescription drugs must have a policy process for insured individuals to\nimmediately obtain an additional 30 day supply or normal refill amount\nof their prescribed medication. Section (c) (1) amends section 4329 to\nmandate that contracts issued by corporations cover these same events.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends section 4329 of the insurance law by adding\nsection (c). Section (c).2 allows the commissioner of health to promul-\ngate a list of prescription drugs that will not be subject section (c)\n(1). This list will include, but not be limited to, all schedule II and\nschedule III controlled substances.\n\nSection 3 of the law is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen preparing for a disaster individuals are advised to stock up on\nimportant supplies such as food, water, and basic supplies. This\nincludes over the counter medication and prescribed medication that they\nmight need to live or maintain quality of life. Prescriptions are typi-\ncally refilled monthly; however, in an emergency situation it may be\ndifficult for people to access their prescription.\n\nDuring a state disaster insurance policies may not provide their custom-\ners with a process to obtain their prescriptions. This places many New\nYorkers at risk of not having their necessary prescription medication\nduring an emergency. Inflexible policies will compound the effect of a\ndisaster by leaving many New Yorkers without their necessary\nprescription medication.\n\nThis bill will ensure declared emergencies are not made worse by requir-\ning insurance companies to enable their customers to obtain an addi-\ntional 30-day supply of their prescribed medication.\n\nAbuse protections are included in the legislation to prevent abuse of\ncertain drugs because the commissioner of health can exclude certain\ndrugs, including schedule II and Ill controlled substances.\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S7989 - referred to Insurance\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to all policies\nand contracts issued, renewed, modified, altered or amended on or after\nsuch date.\n",
